  "verbalization": "Neomycin is linked to KB through a series of molecular pathways involving hepatocytes. The evidence suggests that neomycin acts as a target on hepatocytes, which are the primary cellular targets of the drug. The specific molecular entities involved—such as (1R,2R,4S,7Z,11R)-4,8-dimethyl-12-methylidene-3,14-dioxatricyclo[9.3.0.02,4]tetradec-7-en-13-one, (1S,4S,4aR,8aS)-4-[(E)-5-hydroxy-3-methylpent-3-enyl]-4a,8,8-trimethyl-3-methylidene-2,4,5,6,7,8a-hexahydro-1H-naphthalen-1-ol, and (5R,7S,8S)-5-hydroxy-4-methoxy-7,8-dimethyl-7,8-dihydro-5H-pyrano[4,3-b]pyran-2-one—suggest that neomycin interacts with these compounds in the hepatocyte context, potentially through a common mechanism of action. The shared relationship with KB implies that these compounds may be part of a broader class of molecules that neomycin targets, possibly through a common pathway or a distinct mechanism. The evidence supports a direct interaction between neomycin and KB, mediated by hepatocytes, and highlights the importance of these molecular entities in the drug's biological activity.",
